      {
        "id": "LAW:V21:HP:VESTING",
        "text": "The Appellate Court was clearly in error in not appreciating that \n<ji by s. 3 of the Acquisition Act, the right, title and interest of Esso \nEastern In6. in relation to its undertakings in India, shall stand trans­\nferred to, and sh~ll vest in, the Central Government as from the \nappointed ~ay i.e. as from 13th March, 1\\174. Under sub-s. ( 1) of s~ 5 \nthereof, the Central Government became the lessee or tenant, as the \ncase may be. By sub·s. (2J thereof, on the expiry of the term of any \nM · lease or tenancy referred to in sub-s. (1), such lease or tenancy shall, if \n' \n' \nHINDUSTAN PETROLEUM CORPN. v. HOUSING SOCIETY {SEN, J.i 59 \nso desired by the Central Government, be renewed on the same terms \nand conditions on which the lease or tenancy was held by Esso \nimmediately before the appointed day. By a notification issued on the \nnext date, the right, title and interest of the Central Government \nbecame vested in Esso Standard Refining Company of India Ltd., a \nGovernment company, w.e.f. 15th March, 1974. Furthermore, by \nreason of Lube India and Esso Standard Refining Company°of India \nLtd. Amalgamation Order, 1974 made by the Company Law Board \nunder s. 39611.\\ & (2) of the Companies Act, 1956, the undertaking of \nLube lndia Ltd. vested ir, Esso Standard Refining Company of India \nLtd. and immediately upon sucli transfer, the name of Esso Standard \nRefining Company of India Ltd., stood changed to Hindustan \nfetroleum Corporation Ltd. \nIn the premises, petitioner No. 1 Hindustan PetroleumCorpo­\nration Ltd. is clearly protected under s. lSA of the Bombay Rents, \nHotel and Lodging House Rates Control Act, 1947. In that view of the \nmatter, we do not think it necessary to deal with the contention as \nregards the applicability of s. 9) of the Maharashtra Co-operative \nSocieties Act, 1960. All aspects arising out of the submissions as to the \njurisdiction of the Registrar under s. 91(1) of the Act have already \nbeen considered by this Court on O.N. Bhatnagar's case and we reit­\nerate the principles laid down therein. \n",
